<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emmanuel Endeley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1916–1988</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emmanuel Endeley was a Cameroonian physician and politician who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-cameroons">Southern Cameroons</a> in British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroons</a>. He headed the territory's government from 1954 and became its first premier in 1958, favouring close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. He lost the 1959 election to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ngu-foncha">John Ngu Foncha</a>, whose party backed reunification with Cameroon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emmanuel-endeley.jpg" alt="Emmanuel Endeley" width="148" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prabook · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E._M._L._Endeley.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
